WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:06.559
This is how you contain the worst nuclear
disaster in humanity's history. This is the

00:00:06.559 --> 00:00:12.720
New Safe Confinement structure, at Reactor
4 at Chernobyl.

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:18.140
In 1986, the nuclear power plant here exploded
-- and that is a sentence you never ever want

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:23.470
to hear. The local area was evacuated, and
it won't be safe to live in for millennia:

00:00:23.470 --> 00:00:27.249
to get here you need special permissions,
although it's become a bit easier in recent

00:00:27.249 --> 00:00:28.569
years.

00:00:28.569 --> 00:00:33.090
In the months after the disaster, a concrete
sacrophagus -- it's called the Shelter Object

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:38.930
-- was built around the damaged reactor. It
was constructed rapidly and desperately. It

00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:44.380
cost a lot of lives, and used a megaton of
concrete and metal. It worked, but it won't

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:49.290
hold forever, and it was built on top of the
unstable debris of the old reactor.

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:54.880
But over there is its replacement, still under
construction. It's the New Safe Confinement,

00:00:54.880 --> 00:01:01.300
and it has cost more than one and a half billion
US dollars. It is enormous, a steel arch a

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:07.110
hundred metres high. When it's done, it'll
be hauled by steel cables on enormous tracks

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:12.390
over the reactor over the course of a week.
And once it's there: the sides will be filled

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:17.470
in, and the reactor will be contained again,
ready to be decommissioned safely. This is

00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:21.510
the world's largest movable structure -- although
it's only going to have to move once.

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:25.800
And it's got some pretty astonishing design
requirements. It can't let anything leak out.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:29.830
And it has to withstand everything that Ukraine's
weather can possibly throw at it for a hundred

00:01:29.830 --> 00:01:31.190
years.

00:01:31.190 --> 00:01:34.190
But just think about that. One and a half
billion dollars for something that'll last

00:01:34.190 --> 00:01:39.090
a hundred years. That's great -- provided
in that hundred years, we can find the money,

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:44.850
time and technology to safely dispose of all
the radioactive waste that's going to be in

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:49.450
there. Will we do that? Or, once there's not
an imminent threat of disaster, will we kick

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:51.890
it down the road for a future generation to
deal with?

00:01:51.890 --> 00:01:57.720
In a hundred years, when this arch is at the
end of its lifespan, will we be dismantling

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:04.010
it and celebrating a job well done? Or will
we be spending another huge sum building its replacement?

00:02:04.010 --> 00:02:06.010
